            "kind": "TRIVIAL_REBASE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Just to double check, this wouldn't go into redfish, right?\n\nRedfish already has recommendations for how to put a CPER into a Log (PlatformError).  We shouldn't be inventing Redfish OEM things for things that exist in the standard.\n\nSo is the thinking we would log one of these, then we would log something from Platform registry?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gen/xyz/openbmc_project/State/meson.build",
                    "line": 4,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Would it make sense to put this in Boot?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "gen/xyz/openbmc_project/State/meson.build",
                    "line": 4,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Not sure I'm convinced either way, but I figure it's worth asking."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/CPER.events.yaml",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jayanth Othayoth",
                        "email": "ojayanth@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ojayanth"
                    },
                    "message": "Based on CPER documenation\n Indicates the severity of the error condition. The severity of the error record corresponds to the most severe error section.\n0 - Recoverable (also called non-fatal uncorrected)\n1 - Fatal\n2 - Corrected\n3 - Informational\nAll other values are reserved.\nNote that severity of \u201cInformational\u201d indicates that the record could be safely ignored by error handling software It will be good to start with these events?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/CPER.events.yaml",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "@ojayanth@gmail.com are you indicating that we might need more than 2 event types here one for each error level? \nFatal - GenericCPERFault\nRecoverable - GenericCPERWarning\nCorrected/Informational - need new mapping?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/CPER.events.yaml",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jayanth Othayoth",
                        "email": "ojayanth@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ojayanth"
                    },
                    "message": "It is better to align with Standard CPER format mentioned here. https://uefi.org/specs/UEFI/2.10/Apx_N_Common_Platform_Error_Record.html#record-header"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/CPER.events.yaml",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "FYI, this is not generating CPER events. It is a vehicle to carry CPER events generated externally out of redfish on a well known message ID. The `CPER` string is what's going to contain the CPER data which will of course be the format you expect it to be in (From [libcper](https://github.com/openbmc/libcper))."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/CPER.events.yaml",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> carry CPER events generated externally out of redfish\n\nThen it's the incorrect vehicle.  Redfish has examples of CPER messages that use PlatformError from the platform registry.  Unless we want to start a discussion in a DMTF forum either public or private (which we can and is already progressing) we shouldn't directly oppose examples by doing an OEM thing.  That bifurcates industry support in a bad way.  If we as OpenBMC want to define some dbus interface that doesn't hit Redfish and isn't constrained by its release schedule, dbus-rest is always an option.\n\n> The CPER string is what's going to contain the CPER data which will of course be the format you expect it to be in\n\nWhich.... this data wouldn't be available in Redfish, correct?  There's already a DTMF discussion about doing libcper-style decoding within Redfish that we shouldn't branch our own OpenBMC specific version from."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/CPER.events.yaml",
                    "line": 1,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jayanth Othayoth",
                        "email": "ojayanth@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ojayanth"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/CPER.events.yaml",
                    "line": 4,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "What's the difference between warning and fault?  I suspect we probably want them the same for this initial example, and differentiate based on severity?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/CPER.events.yaml",
                    "line": 4,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "@ed@tanous.net Looking at https://www.dmtf.org/sites/default/files/standards/documents/DSP2065_2025.1.pdf, the PlatformError and PlatformErrorAtLocation messages have the severity set as `Severity: Warning`. So, using anything other than this is breaking the schema.\n\nThats the reason why we are adding this in order to differentiate between different CPER severity types."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/CPER.events.yaml",
                    "line": 4,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> . So, using anything other than this is breaking the schema.\n\nThat's not how Redfish registries work, but this is the wrong forum to discuss redfish protocol specifics.  If you want more details about severity when used with a message registry, DMTF github or redfish forum (dependent on whether or not you want your message public) is the right place to discuss."
                }
            ],
